Solar energy is one of the quickest growing sources of electricity in the world. Photovoltaic panels convert sunlight directly into electrical current, while concentrated solar plants use mirrors to heat a fluid and drive a turbine. Over the last decade, the cost of panels has fallen sharply, making solar competitive with coal and natural gas in many regions. Storage remains the primary challenge, because the sun does not shine at night. Batteries and pumped hydro are being deployed to smooth out supply. Governments continue to offer incentives to homeowners who install rooftop systems, and large utility-scale farms now cover thousands of hectares in sunny countries.